        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Thracians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, with north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>; key sites <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a>, Perperikon, Tatul, Starosel, the Valley of the Thracian Rulers with the Tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak">Kazanlak</a>, the Tomb of Sveshtari</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Thracian culture emerged in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>; first named in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>; Greek colonies on its coasts from the 8th century BCE; Odrysian Kingdom from the early 5th century BCE; Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a> from 46 CE; last reported use of a Thracian language in the 6th century CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">skilled horsemen and peltasts; the rhomphaia; gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> treasures such as Panagyurishte and Rogozen; the cult of the Thracian horseman; early worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>; tattooing; poetry and music; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Greek and Roman writers (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>), Persian sources and reliefs, four short Thracian inscriptions, tombs, sanctuaries and treasures studied by Thracology, DNA studies</span></div>
