    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>‘Thracians’ was the Greek name for some 200 tribes of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> who shared much of their culture but were rarely united in one state.</li><li>Greek and Roman writers called them warlike barbarians while hiring them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and noting their poetry and music; the fairness of these accounts is now questioned.</li><li>Their tombs and gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> treasures, studied mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, give a fuller picture of a people gradually absorbed into Roman, Byzantine and Slavic societies.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 570 CE Antoninus Placentius reported a monastery in the valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> whose monks spoke Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Egyptian and Bessian, the language of a Thracian tribe.</p></aside>
    </section>

