    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first Greek colonies are founded on the Thracian coasts</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">637 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Thracian Treres, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycians">Lycians</a>, capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, the Lydian capital, except its citadel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">513 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> marches into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and brings its tribes under Persian rule</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">early 5th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teres-i">Teres I</a> founds the Odrysian Kingdom, the first lasting Thracian state</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">73–71 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Thracian gladiator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a> leads a great slave uprising in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">46 CE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a> becomes a Roman province after the murder of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhoemetalces-iii">Rhoemetalces III</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">570 CE</span><span class="rf-text">Antoninus Placentius records monks speaking Bessian at a monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1960s–1970s</span><span class="rf-text">Intensive excavations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> uncover many Thracian tombs and sanctuaries</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/homer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="Homer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homer_At_the_British_Museum_2024_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net)., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>“Thracians” is the name the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> gave to many peoples of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. It comes from the Greek Thrāix, and both it and the name of their land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, are exonyms, names given by outsiders; according to Stephanus of Byzantium the land was once called Perki and Aria. The first record of the name is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, where Thracians from the land bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> fight as allies of the Trojans; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> is believed to have taken place around the 12th century BCE. In Greek myth Thrax, the quintessential Thracian, was a son of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>, who was regarded as Thrace’s patron.</p><p>Thracian origins are obscure, because the Thracians left no written records before they met the Greeks. It is generally proposed that they developed from a mixture of indigenous peoples and Indo-Europeans during the Proto-Indo-European expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and like other Indo-European-speaking groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> they are believed to descend from Proto-Indo-Europeans and Early European Farmers. During the 5th and 4th millennia BCE the inhabitants of the eastern Balkans became organised into groups that the Greeks later called Thracians; Thracian culture emerged in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Age and flourished in the 3rd and 2nd millennia BCE. Leo Klejn identifies the proto-Thracians with the multi-cordoned ware culture, pushed out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> by the advancing Srubnaya culture.</p><p>Accounts differ on how united they were. The Thracians spoke the now-extinct Thracian language and shared a common culture, but one view holds that they did not all speak the same language, the varied landscape preventing a single one from forming. They were split into many tribes, about 200 of which have been identified, living across most of the Balkans, from the Getae north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> in the west, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii.jpg" alt="Seuthes III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Archaeological_Museum_Sofia_-_Bronze_Head_from_the_Golyama_Kosmatka_Tumulus_near_Shipka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ann Wuyts, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most Thracians lived in simple, open villages and were not city-builders: their only polis was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a>, the capital founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a> around 330 BCE. They were divided into a great number of tribes, and ancient historians held that only this constant political fragmentation stopped them overrunning the lands of the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> called them the second most numerous people he knew of, after the Indians, and potentially the most powerful but for their lack of unity.</p><p>Several powerful states did form. The Odrysian Kingdom, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teres-i">Teres I</a> in the early 5th century BCE, was a union of more than 40 Thracian tribes and 22 kingdoms, centred on present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. In the 1st century BCE the powerful Dacian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> emerged. Greek writers describe Thracian men as polygamous, and in Menander a speaker says that no Thracian takes fewer than ten, eleven or twelve wives, while Herodotus claims that unmarried Thracian women were free to choose their partners. Some peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> were vegetarian, living on honey, milk and cheese.</p><p>War shaped Thracian society. They were known as skilled horsemen and as peltasts, lightly armed skirmishers with javelins, and their soldiers were valued as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, particularly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Noblemen were buried with their horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> so that these could serve them again in the afterlife. Thracians were also among the main victims of Greek slave raids: of 32 slaves of known origin confiscated from the men who mutilated the Hermai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, 13 came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>’ comedies a slave woman is simply called Thratta, “Thracian woman”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dionysus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dionysus.jpg" alt="Dionysus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dionysos_Louvre_Ma87_n2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Thracians followed a polytheistic religion with some monotheistic elements. A cult found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> and Getae honoured the “Thracian horseman”, also known as Sabazios or the Thracian Heros, by a Thracian name Heros Karabazmos, a god of the underworld usually shown on funeral monuments as a rider slaying a beast with a spear. The Getae and Dacians may have had a monotheistic religion centred on the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalmoxis">Zalmoxis</a>, though this is heavily debated. The Balkan thunder god Perkon belonged to the Thracian pantheon, although the cults of Orpheus and Zalmoxis likely overshadowed him.</p><p>The Thracians are considered the first to worship the god of wine, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> in Greek and Zagreus in Thracian, whose cult later reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and some regard Thrace as the motherland of wine culture. In myth Orpheus, chief among poets and musicians, was a king of the Thracian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicones">Cicones</a>. In classical times celibate ascetics called ctistae lived in Thrace as philosophers, priests and prophets, held in honour and dedicated to the gods.</p><p>Other customs are known from outside accounts and archaeology. Tattooing was common among both men and women. The French historian Victor Duruy wrote that the Thracians practised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, which archaeological evidence has confirmed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> came late: towards the end of the 4th century CE Nicetas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Remesiana, preached to the Bessi, and Christianity eventually replaced the worship of Dionysus and the other Thracian gods.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thracian craftsmanship is best known from the elaborate gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> treasures of the 5th and 4th centuries BCE, such as the Panagyurishte, Rogozen, Valchitran, Borovo and Lukovit treasures, which have been exhibited in museums around the world. Excavations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> have uncovered tombs and sanctuaries such as the Tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak">Kazanlak</a> in the Valley of the Thracian Rulers, the Getic burial complex and the Tomb of Sveshtari, and the sites of Tatul and Perperikon. Greek and Roman writers, who thought the Thracians unsophisticated, still noted the sophistication of their poetry and music.</p><p>Thracian writing is very scarce: only four inscriptions have been found. One, on a gold ring unearthed in the village of Ezerovo in Bulgaria, has eight lines written in the Greek script, but attempts to decipher it have been inconclusive.</p><p>Weapons show special skill. Well-preserved bronze fencing swords from as early as the 16th century BCE have been found across Bulgaria and suggest that duelling was common. The rhomphaia, a two-handed polearm with a long, slightly curved blade, was the deadliest Thracian weapon, especially effective against cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and the sica was a curved short sword. High-ranking soldiers of the archaic period wore helmets of boar tusks; later helmet styles were adapted to give riders better all-round vision and ease of movement, and Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> adopted the Thracian cavalry’s wedge-shaped formation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/odrysians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odrysians.jpg" alt="Odrysians" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odrysian.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexikoua, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Thracians lived among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>. Greek colonies were founded along their coasts from the 8th century BCE, first on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, then on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and Thracians and Greeks lived side by side. In 637 BCE the Thracian Treres, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycians">Lycians</a>, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and took its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, apart from its citadel, until Scythians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madyes">Madyes</a> drove them out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><p>From 513 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> marched through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Many tribes, including the Odrysae, submitted, and the Getae south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> were defeated. Thrace became part of the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of Skudra, and Thracians served in the Persian army in the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>. Persian authority in central Thrace lasted until about 465 BCE, after which the Odrysian Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> filled the gap. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a> were long allies of Athens and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> on its side; in the 4th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> conquered southern Thrace, and Thracian cavalry and javelin men later served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Issus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a>.</p><p>Celts advanced into Thrace in 279 BCE and remained until the end of the 3rd century BCE; three Celtic tribes went on to found Galatia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Battle of Pydna</a> in 168 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took over the governance of Thrace, and the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a> in 149 BCE drew most of its support from Thrace. A Roman client kingdom under the Sapaei followed, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a> became a Roman province after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhoemetalces-iii">Rhoemetalces III</a> was murdered in 46 CE. Earlier, the Thracian gladiator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a> had led a great slave rising in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 73–71 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax.jpg" alt="Maximinus Thrax" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax">Maximinus Thrax</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maximinus_Thrax_Musei_Capitolini_MC473.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Repeated invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, Sarmatians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenization">Hellenisation</a>, Romanisation and later Slavicisation, reshaped the ethnicity, language and culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Writers still spoke of a separate Thracian nationality into the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the last reported use of a Thracian language was by monks in the 6th century CE. Several Roman and Byzantine emperors were wholly or partly of Thracian descent, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax">Maximinus Thrax</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> and Leo I the Thracian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> was born to a Thracian father of the Moesi tribe.</p><p>Most likely the Thracians were assimilated into Roman and Byzantine society and became one of the ancestral groups of modern south-eastern Europeans. Those not yet assimilated were incorporated into the Slavic-speaking Bulgarian Empire; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a> in 680 CE Thracians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and Slavs united in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, and the three groups mingled to produce the Bulgarian people. Gottfried Schramm speculated that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> descend from the Thracian Bessi, but there is no archaeological or linguistic support for this. Genetic studies suggest that about 55% of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>’ ancestry is of Paleo-Balkan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> origin, attributed to Thracians, Anatolians and other peoples of the Balkans before the Slavs and Bulgars.</p><p>The study of the Thracians is called Thracology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> have used archaeology to learn about them since the 19th and early 20th centuries, and intensive excavations in the 1960s and 1970s, mainly in southern Bulgaria, uncovered many tombs and sanctuaries. Since 2000 Georgi Kitov has made discoveries in the “Valley of the Thracian Kings” in central Bulgaria, including the residence of the Odrysian kings at Starosel, and a project begun in Bulgaria in 2016 brought together 72 scholars to study Thracian heritage.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Thracians were Indo-European-speaking peoples of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, centred on present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> named as one people though they lived in some 200 tribes. Famed as horsemen and peltasts, they made rich gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> work, worshipped the Thracian horseman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, and formed states such as the Odrysian Kingdom. Under Persian, Macedonian and Roman rule they were gradually absorbed into the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</em></p>

